Exactly right. That is the perfect core logic for testing an IDOR.
By keeping your own Bearer token but changing the target_uid to your second account's ID (8843), you are testing whether the server actually validates ownership, or if it just blindly trusts the ID provided in the JSON body.
To round out your understanding of this specific scenario, let's look at the two potential outcomes when you send that modified request:
• The Secure Outcome (No Bug): The server returns a 401 Unauthorized or 403 Forbidden error. It successfully checked your token, realized you don't own UID 8843, and blocked the request.
• The Vulnerable Outcome (Valid Bug): The server returns a 200 OK or 204 No Content, and when you check your second account, its profile data has been changed. This proves the server only validated that your token was logged in, but forgot to check if you had permission to edit that specific UID.

🛡️ Going Deeper: Handling UUIDs

Many modern platforms like OFZA try to prevent basic IDORs by using UUIDs (Universally Unique Identifiers) instead of predictable numbers like 8842.
A UUID looks like this: f81d4fae-7dec-11d0-a765-00a0c91e6bf6
Because these are virtually impossible to guess by brute-force, developers sometimes assume they are completely secure. However, a UUID-based endpoint can still be vulnerable to IDOR if an attacker can find or leak another user's UUID.
On a crypto exchange, look for places where a user's UUID might be exposed publicly or to other users:
• Public order books (showing who placed a bid).
• Community chat features or forums.
• Peer-to-peer (P2P) trading boards where users list offers.
If you can grab a second account's UUID from a public page, you can plug it into your authorization tests just like you did with the numerical ID!

🚀 Module 2: Business Logic & Race Conditions

Since you have mastered the concept of IDOR, let's move to Module 2, which is highly relevant to financial and crypto platforms.

1. The Concept

A Race Condition happens when a web application processes requests sequentially, but a multi-threaded system tries to do multiple things at the exact same fraction of a second. If the application checks a condition (like "Does this user have enough money?") and then performs an action (like "Deduct money and send crypto"), a rapid burst of concurrent requests can slip through between the check and the deduction.

2. The Crypto Context

On exchanges, attackers look for race conditions in high-stakes actions:
• Withdrawals: Trying to withdraw $100 five times simultaneously when you only have $100 in your balance. If the code processes the requests faster than it updates your database balance, you might successfully withdraw $500.
• Coupon/Promo Codes: Redeeming a single-use sign-up bonus multiple times at once.
• Order Executions: Trying to cancel an order at the exact millisecond it gets filled to see if you get both the refunded assets and the traded assets.

3. How to Test Manually

You cannot test this by clicking a button quickly with your mouse. You need a tool to send requests at the exact same millisecond.
1. Capture the target request (e.g., POST /api/wallet/withdraw) in Burp Suite.
2. Send the request to the Repeater tool.
3. Duplicate the request tab 10 to 20 times.
4. Create a Request Group in Burp Suite, add all those tabs to it, and select "Send group (parallel)". This instructs Burp to blast all the requests over the network at the exact same instant.
